    <GaugingStation location="Dotton" name="Otter" number="45005">
        <Comments>
            <Comment type="Station Summary">Velocity-area station opened in 1962 and rebuilt after 1968 flood, weir built in 1971.</Comment>
            <Comment type="Hydrometric">Natural control up until weir installed in 1971. Now considered a full range station. Low embankments at field level extend containment. Gabions stabilise bed and banks. Bypassing occurs during exceptional events (&gt;2.1m). Multiple ratings applied across period of record; high flow events in Nov/Dec 2000 altered channel geometry u/s and d/s of station. Ratings after this period include estimates for out of bank flow.</Comment>
            <Comment type="General">Velocity area station with cableway. Station opened in 10/1962. Hut rebuilt after July 1968 flood. 14m wide informal shallow Flat V weir installed 1971. Initially for Water Resources, more recently considered full range with FCRM interest.</Comment>
            <Comment type="Flow record">Full period of record peak flow data reviewed and released in September 2020 (WINFAP Files v9).</Comment>
            <Comment type="Flow regime">Substantial groundwater and surface water abstractions in catchment for public water supply and industrial/agricultural purposes. Flows increased by effluent returns.</Comment>
            <Comment type="Catchment">Rises in Greensand and Gault Clay of the Blackdown Hills. Predominantly Keuper sandstones and marls. Extensive alluvium and valley gravels lower down. Some heathland, woodland and pasture, and a wide range of agriculture. No major catchment changes.</Comment>
            <Comment type="Factors affecting runoff">PGEI</Comment>
        </Comments>
        <Usages>
            <Usage notes="Good fit to gaugings." suitable="Yes" type="QMED"/>
            <Usage notes="Gauged close to AMAX3. Pre-2000 ratings do not take out of bank flow into account and therefore flows are underestimated." suitable="No" type="Pooling"/>
        </Usages>
    </GaugingStation>
